      note: "CLOSED — NO MECHANISM EXISTS (caiso-143, kill-before-solve, no run, no field, keeper unchanged). Chartered as the first of the two prerequisites caiso-142 §J said any future export-sink arm needs. TWO independent refusals. (1) REDUNDANT: the priced per-hub corridor nodes carry load_share = 0.000, exactly ONE link out and ZERO links in, and their solved slack = dump = 0.0000 MW in every hour of every year in BOTH committed bundles (caiso139_dumpguard_B and caiso142_seam_B), so the node's energy balance IS the exact identity sum_g P[g,t] == link_flow[t] and a row bounding the node's NET export interchange is the SAME linear combination as the corridor-group bound ALREADY armed (group limit_dn == measured_corridor_flow_envelope(direction='export'), max diff 0.000000 MW over all 6 corridor-years). caiso-142 §J measured the consequence from the other side: net link flow >= 0 in 100.0% of the resale hours, i.e. the chartered row is SLACK in precisely the hours it was chartered to bind and drives the sink to 0 in NONE of the 99/1,463/734/1,422/1,393/948 hours. Separately, the cited machinery (rows.py:1416) builds ONE ROW PER MONTH pinning a monthly net-throughput BAND (the NYISO EIA-930 reconciliation design), not an hourly directional bound. (2) NOT LP-REPRESENTABLE AT ALL: soundness (every MWh the sink books as an export is energy that actually left, -S <= max(0, -flow)) defines a NON-CONVEX set — flow=+2,100/S=0 and flow=-500/S=-500 are both sound, their midpoint flow=+800/S=-250 is RESALE — and an LP feasible region is convex by construction, so no rows at any granularity (hourly, monthly, node, link, grouped, net or gross) can express it; the exact condition is the disjunction {flow>=0 => S=0} OR {flow<0 => -S<=-flow}, which needs a binary (MIP, forbidden by the stack rules); its tightest LINEAR surrogate -S <= -flow reduces via the node identity to F + I_econ <= 0, INFEASIBLE while the must-flow block F > 0 (2 of 9 injection rows are price-insensitive and force 11.668 + 15.564 TWh in 2024); and its convex HULL contains the resale points. The one non-redundant linear form, a GROSS absorption column bound S >= -envelope, is ALSO refused: it leaves resale in 31.7-51.4% (PNW) / 7.4-39.8% (DSW) of transacting hours — exactly the hours the must-flow block alone equals or exceeds the corridor's measured export capability, with the PNW 2024/2025 net-flow floor POSITIVE (+67.6/+46.4 MW) so the corridor stays a net importer at full permitted absorption — while making the pre-registered caiso-142 P2 gate (the ONLY gate that detected the defect) pass BY CONSTRUCTION, which is rule 1 [R-STRUCT]'s 'never reach the right number through a mechanism that is not real'. Bounded-arm price prediction if armed anyway: C3a +2.296/+2.648/+0.957, i.e. 3.8x C3a-2024's remaining $0.69 band headroom — it leaves the band exactly as the unconstrained arm did. THE DEPENDENCY INVERTS: caiso-138 §C's forced-injection elasticity is prerequisite (a), not (c), because with F = 0 the resale channel is precluded by the OBJECTIVE (§B's wheel arithmetic: buy-to-resell always loses) so nothing is left to constrain, and the already-armed net bound still bounds the physical flow — NOT because the surrogate becomes usable, since at F = 0 it collapses to I_econ = 0 and would forbid legitimate imports too. CROSS-ISO PRECONDITION CENSUSED on a SECOND axis (§G, measurement only, rule 25 — caiso-142 §F censused the BRIDGE axis 'does the seam delete the sink?', this censuses the RESALE axis 'do an absorption row and a must-flow injection meet at one node?'). The channel needs BOTH halves at one priced node; they disagree in three places. PJM and NEISO are NOT exposed on the resale axis despite 40 and 3 absorption rows — they arm NO must-flow import injector, and §B shows an all-economic node cannot resell at any sink price (economic tranche costs hub + wheel + eps against the sink's hub - eps). CAISO (WECC_DSW 1 sink + 7 injects, WECC_PNW 1 + 2) and NYISO (NYISO_external 1 + 7 incl. HQ_hydro, nyiso_firm_imports armed) DO meet it, but both have a P1-native bridge armed so their sinks are pinned off — the channel is currently closed BY THE SEAM BUG and their signed seam totals never go negative (0 h, all years); for NYISO this inverts the usual reading, since ARMING the seam fix there would OPEN the channel, so the pinned state is the safer of the two until its own lane decides. MISO IS THE ONE ISO WHERE THE CHANNEL IS STRUCTURALLY OPEN IN THE SCORED KEEPER TODAY: MISO_external carries 24 live sinks alongside 24 injects including the ref_import_Manitoba_t* must-flow block (miso_firm_imports armed), MISO_external_South 8 + 8, NO P1-native bridge to delete them, and its signed seam total goes negative in 29/577/1,182 hours (2023/24/25, rising) — so caiso-142 §F's 'latent' label is wrong for MISO on this axis. What that shows and does NOT show: the precondition is met and the sinks are live; whether the channel is EXERCISED (gross absorption against same-node injection, as caiso-142 §J measured for CAISO) needs unit-level rows its slim bundle does not carry, so that measurement is MISO's lane's to make. Cells: ERCOT n/a (0 interchange rows, caiso-142 §F); PJM/MISO/NYISO/NEISO U — the topology argument is general but verdicts are per-ISO (rule 25), so each lane derives its own, MISO included.",
